/**
 * @defgroup Foundation_Workflows Workflows
 * @ingroup Foundation_BuildGuide
 * @brief Repeatable configure, build, test, install, export, and maintenance workflows.
 *
 * Foundation uses CMake presets to describe a target and small repository
 * scripts to execute a workflow. The scripts do not contain platform-specific
 * compiler logic. A preset selects the platform, architecture, compiler,
 * generator, and cross-compilation toolchain. Debug or Release is selected by
 * the workflow and is deliberately not part of the preset identity.
 *
 * Bash scripts are provided for macOS, Linux, and compatible shells. Matching
 * PowerShell scripts provide the same operations on Windows. Every script
 * resolves the repository root from its own location, so it can be invoked from
 * any working directory.
 *
 * @section FoundationWorkflowsResponsibilities Responsibilities
 *
 * The build system is split into four layers:
 *
 * - `CMakePresets.json` and `cmake/presets/` define supported artifact
 *   identities and reproducible target defaults;
 * - `cmake/toolchains/` defines cross-compilers, CPU flags, ABI flags, target
 *   runtimes, and program lookup rules;
 * - CMake cache options select Foundation modules or machine-local SDK paths;
 * - `scripts/` performs one operation consistently without duplicating target
 *   knowledge.
 *
 * A workflow script is an interface to CMake, not a second build system. When a
 * target changes, update its preset or toolchain. When the sequence of CMake
 * operations changes, update the script.
 *
 * @section FoundationWorkflowsDirectories Workflow Directories
 *
 * @code
 * build/<preset>/   Temporary CMake cache and build artifacts
 * dist/<preset>/    Installed, redistributable package
 * docs/Workflows/   Source documentation for these workflows
 * @endcode
 *
 * Build directories are disposable. Distribution directories are created only
 * by install or export operations and must remain separated by target preset.
 * One package may contain distinct Debug and Release libraries, but compiler,
 * architecture, ABI, and feature-set changes require a different package or a
 * deliberate custom re-export.
 *
 * @section FoundationWorkflowsState Cached State
 *
 * `configure`, `build`, `test`, and `export` reuse `build/<preset>` unless their
 * fresh option is supplied. This is convenient during development, but cache
 * overrides not explicitly replaced by the preset or script can remain active.
 * The test workflow enables testing only; examples remain independent. Use a
 * fresh export for release candidates so the package starts from preset defaults
 * plus only the overrides shown in the release command.
 *
 * @section FoundationWorkflowsSequence Normal Release Sequence
 *
 * @code{bash}
 * ./scripts/configure.sh macos_arm64 --fresh
 * ./scripts/build.sh macos_arm64 --config Debug --parallel 8
 * ./scripts/test.sh macos_arm64 --config Debug --parallel 8
 * ./scripts/export.sh macos_arm64 --fresh --parallel 8
 * @endcode
 *
 * @warning The test step currently stops this sequence because no CTest cases
 * are registered. That is an intentional release-gate failure, not permission
 * to silently skip testing. See @ref Foundation_Workflows_Test.
 *
 * The Windows equivalent uses the matching `.ps1` scripts. Cross-compiled
 * targets do not run host-side CTest executables; follow
 * @ref Foundation_Workflows_Validation for their compile, export, architecture,
 * and firmware-consumer checks.
 *
 * Start with @ref Foundation_Workflows_Manual for the complete local workflow.
 * See the other subgroups for each command's contract, defaults, side effects,
 * expected output, and failure modes.
 */

